canonical_claim.statement:
Printed. PDF p. 14: 'Theorem 4. Suppose $w$ satisfies Assumption 3. Let $\mathcal S$ be the set of all pairs of distributions $\mathcal D,\mathcal D'$ such that the distributions of $X_i,Y_i(1)$ remain unchanged between $\mathcal D$ and $\mathcal D'$. If for all pairs of distributions $\mathcal D,\mathcal D'\in\mathcal S$, $E_{\mathcal D}[w(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=E_{\mathcal D'}[w(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$ for all $\pi$, then regret is unbounded.'

Corrected statement. Suppose the policy $\pi\equiv0$ belongs to $\Pi$, $w$ satisfies Assumption 3, and for every $\pi\in\Pi$ the expected reward $E_{\mathcal D}[w(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$ is the same for any two distributions $\mathcal D,\mathcal D'$ of $(X_i,Y_i(1),Y_i(0))$ with finite means that have the same joint distribution of $(X_i,Y_i(1))$. Then for every $M>0$ there exist a distribution $\mathcal D$ and a best response $\pi_*\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E_{\mathcal D}[w(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$ with $R_{\mathcal D}(\pi_*)>M$. In detail: let $\mathcal D_*$ and $\pi_*\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E_{\mathcal D_*}[w]$ be given by Assumption 3, with $c=E_{\mathcal D_*}[\pi_*(X_i)]>0$, and for $B>0$ let $\mathcal D_B$ keep the joint distribution of $(X_i,Y_i(1))$ under $\mathcal D_*$ and set $Y_i(0)=Y_i(1)+B$. Then:
(i) $\pi_*\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E_{\mathcal D_B}[w(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$;
(ii) under $\mathcal D_B$, $\tau\equiv-B$ and $V_{\mathcal D_B}(\pi)=-B\,E[\pi(X_i)]\leq0$ for every $\pi\in\Pi$, with $\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V_{\mathcal D_B}(\pi)=0$ attained at $\pi\equiv0$;
(iii) $R_{\mathcal D_B}(\pi_*)=Bc$, which exceeds $M$ for $B=(M+1)/c$.
The conclusion cannot be stated for every best response: with $n=1$, $X_1=0$, $\Pi=\{0,1\}$ (treat nobody, treat everybody), $w\equiv0$, $Y_1(1)=1$ and $Y_1(0)=1+B$, every policy is a best response, Assumption 3 and the invariance hold, and the best response $\pi\equiv0$ has regret $0$ for every $B$, while $\pi\equiv1$ has regret $B$.

Printed. PDF p. 6: 'Theorem 2 (Ranking Desiderata Satisfied). Let $P_{X^k}$ be absolutely continuous with respect to $P_{X_0}$, and let $g_k=\frac1{n_k}\frac{dP_{X_0}}{dP_{X^k}}$ be the normalized Radon–Nikodym derivative of the reference distribution $P_{X_0}$ with respect to agent $k$'s covariate distribution $P_{X^k}$. Then setting $w_k$ to be $w^{g_k}_{TT}$ for agent $k$'s treatment population satisfies both ranking properties in Definitions 1 and 2 as long as $\Pi_k$ is unconstrained and treatment effects are nonnegative, $\tau^k(x)\geq0$, for all $k\in\{1,...,K\}$.' PDF p. 20: 'Theorem 2 (Ranking Desiderata Satisfied). Let $P_{X^{(k)}}$ be absolutely continuous with respect to $P_{X_0}$, and let $g_k=\frac1{n_k}\frac{dP_{X_0}}{dP_{X^{(k)}}}$ be the normalized Radon–Nikodym derivative of the reference distribution $P_{X_0}$ with respect to agent $k$'s covariate distribution $P_{X^{(k)}}$. Then $w_k(\mathbf X^{(k)},\mathbf T^{\pi_k},\mathbf Y^{(k)})=\sum_{i=1}^{n_k}(Y_i^{(k)}-\hat\mu_0(X_i^{(k)}))T_i^{\pi_k}g_k(X^{(k)})$ (2) satisfies both ranking properties in Definitions 1 and 2 as long as $\Pi_k$ is unconstrained and treatment effects are nonnegative, $\tau^{(k)}(x)\geq0$, for all $k\in\{1,...,K\}$.'

Corrected statement. 'Let $P_{X_0}$ be absolutely continuous with respect to $P_{X^{(k)}}$, and let $g_k=\frac1{n_k}\frac{dP_{X_0}}{dP_{X^{(k)}}}$ be the normalized Radon–Nikodym derivative of the reference distribution $P_{X_0}$ with respect to agent $k$'s covariate distribution $P_{X^{(k)}}$. Then $w_k(\mathbf X^{(k)},\mathbf T^{\pi_k},\mathbf Y^{(k)})=\sum_{i=1}^{n_k}(Y_i^{(k)}-\hat\mu_0(X_i^{(k)}))T_i^{\pi_k}g_k(X_i^{(k)})$ satisfies both ranking properties in Definitions 1 and 2 as long as $\Pi_k$ is unconstrained and treatment effects are nonnegative, $\tau^{(k)}(x)\geq0$, for all $k\in\{1,...,K\}$.' Here Definitions 1 and 2 are read with each agent's own sample as the argument of its score (target P006-T14); the standing assumptions (S7) and (S9) hold for each agent; $E|\tau^{(k)}(X_0)|<\infty$; and the estimator satisfies Assumption 1' of target P006-T6 for each agent (for every $k$, every $\pi_k$ and every $i$, $E[T_i^{\pi_k}(\hat\mu_0(X_i^{(k)})-\mu_0(X_i^{(k)}))\mid X_i^{(k)}]=0$), which the printed proof uses through 'As shown in the proof of Theorem 1'. In detail, for each $k$ and each $\pi_k:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$:
(i) $E[w_k(\mathbf X^{(k)},\mathbf T^{\pi_k},\mathbf Y^{(k)})]=n_kE[\tau^{(k)}(X_i^{(k)})\pi_k(X_i^{(k)})g_k(X_i^{(k)})]$;
(ii) (change of measure; this is where $P_{X_0}\ll P_{X^{(k)}}$ is used) $n_kE[\tau^{(k)}(X_i^{(k)})\pi_k(X_i^{(k)})g_k(X_i^{(k)})]=E[\tau^{(k)}(X_0)\pi_k(X_0)]$;
(iii) with $\Pi_k$ unconstrained, $\max_{\pi_k\in\Pi_k}E[w_k(\mathbf X^{(k)},\mathbf T^{\pi_k},\mathbf Y^{(k)})]=E[\tau^{(k)}(X_0)\mathbb 1(\tau^{(k)}(X_0)>0)]$;
(iv) (Definition 1; nonnegativity is not needed here) if $\tau^{(j)}(x)\geq\tau^{(k)}(x)$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, then $E[\tau^{(j)}(X_0)\mathbb 1(\tau^{(j)}(X_0)>0)]\geq E[\tau^{(k)}(X_0)\mathbb 1(\tau^{(k)}(X_0)>0)]$;
(v) (Definition 2) if $\tau^{(k)}(x)\geq0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$ and all $k$, then $\max_{\pi_k\in\Pi_k}E[w_k]=E[\tau^{(k)}(X_0)]$, so $E[\tau^{(j)}(X_0)]\geq E[\tau^{(k)}(X_0)]$ implies $\max_{\pi_j\in\Pi_j}E[w_j]\geq\max_{\pi_k\in\Pi_k}E[w_k]$.
If the weights $g_k$ are also to be strictly positive $P_{X^{(k)}}$-almost everywhere (the premise $g(X_i)>0$ of Theorem 1), require $P_{X_0}$ and $P_{X^{(k)}}$ to be mutually absolutely continuous; this is not needed for (i)-(v).

[Integrability qualification of the corrected statement] For every agent $k$, admissible policy $\pi_k$ and unit $i$, the treated residual $T_i^{\pi_k}(\hat\mu_0(X_i^{(k)})-\mu_0(X_i^{(k)}))$ is absolutely integrable, so the conditional expectation in Assumption 1' is ordinary, and each individual weighted reward summand satisfies $E[|(Y_i^{(k)}-\hat\mu_0(X_i^{(k)}))T_i^{\pi_k}g_k(X_i^{(k)})|]<\infty$. No integrability is required at untreated estimator evaluations. Conclusions (i)--(v) are asserted under these qualifications. The preceding unqualified wording is retained as a historical formulation; it is not independently established by the qualified theorem.
